        "# **CAPSTONE REVIEW**\n",
        "\n",
        "## MODULE 5 | LESSON 2: Advanced Non-linearity Applications\n",
        "\n",
        "| | |\n",
        "|:---|:---|\n",
        "|**Reading Time** | 4h |\n",
        "|**Prior Knowledge** | linear regression, time series analysis, machine learning basics, optimization theory, financial derivatives |\n",
        "|**Keywords** | regime-switching models, hidden Markov models, threshold autoregressive models, neural networks, non-linear optimization, support vector machines, market reflexivity, gradient descent, regulatory capital |\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## **Introduction**\n",
        "Non-linearity represents a fundamental challenge in financial engineering – practitioners employ linear models despite substantial evidence of non-linear market dynamics\n",
        "\n",
        "In this lesson, we'll confront this reality head-on. Markets switch regimes without warning (why do we act surprised when volatility clusters?). Trading creates feedback loops that would make a control engineer weep. Neural networks promise to capture these complexities – though at the cost of interpretability that keeps risk managers awake at night.\n",
        "\n",
        "We begin with regime-switching models that at least acknowledge markets have moods. Hidden Markov models let us quantify what every trader knows: calm markets become turbulent, and turbulence (eventually) subsides. Threshold models take this further by linking regime changes to observable triggers. A bit more ambitiously, we'll see how neural networks can model almost any non-linearity you care to imagine – which is both their greatest strength and their most dangerous weakness.\n",
        "\n",
        "It pays to look at market reflexivity carefully. When everyone uses similar models, the models themselves change market behavior. Support vector machines help us detect regime shifts before they fully manifest (sometimes). We'll also examine how regulators are slowly catching up to non-linear risk – though as usual, they're fighting the last war. Finally, we tackle the computational nightmare of optimizing non-convex objectives, where gradient descent variants offer hope but no guarantees.\n",
        "\n",
        "## **1. Regime-Switching Models Using Hidden Markov Models**\n",
        "\n",
        "Markets exhibit state-dependent dynamics with distinct distributional properties across time periods, as evidenced by the structural differences observed between pre-crisis and crisis periods.\n",
        "\n",
        "Regime-switching models formalize this intuition. Where GARCH models (remember Financial Econometrics Module 5, Lesson 2?) assume smooth volatility evolution, regime models acknowledge that markets can snap from one state to another. Think of it as the difference between dimming lights gradually versus flipping a switch.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **1.1 Hidden Markov Model Framework**\n",
        "\n",
        "The brilliance of Hidden Markov Models lies in their honesty – they admit we can't directly observe market regimes. We see returns. We don't see the underlying mood.\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple two-regime model captures the essence:\n",
        "- **Regime 1 (Bull Market)**: $r_t = \\mu_1 + \\sigma_1 \\epsilon_t$\n",
        "- **Regime 2 (Bear Market)**: $r_t = \\mu_2 + \\sigma_2 \\epsilon_t$\n",
        "\n",
        "Note that $\\mu_1 > \\mu_2$ (bulls are optimistic) and typically $\\sigma_1 < \\sigma_2$ (fear is more volatile than greed). The regime indicator $S_t \\in \\{1, 2\\}$ follows a Markov chain – yesterday's regime influences today's, but last week's doesn't matter once we know yesterday's.\n",
        "\n",
        "The transition matrix captures regime persistence:\n",
        "$$P = \\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & p_{12} \\\\ p_{21} & p_{22} \\end{pmatrix}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $p_{ij} = P(S_{t+1} = j | S_t = i)$. Large diagonal elements mean regimes persist (markets have momentum). Small diagonals suggest frequent switching – a bipolar market that would concern any psychiatrist.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **1.2 Estimation and Inference**\n",
        "\n",
        "Here's where it gets tricky. We observe returns but not regimes – like inferring someone's mood from their spending patterns (possible, but imperfect).\n",
        "\n",
        "The Expectation-Maximization algorithm offers an elegant if computationally intensive solution. First, we guess the regimes given parameters (E-step). Then we estimate parameters given our regime guesses (M-step). This circular logic actually converges – though to local rather than global optima.\n",
        "\n",
        "A bit more precisely:\n",
        "1. **E-step**: Use the Hamilton filter to compute $P(S_t = j | \\text{data})$\n",
        "2. **M-step**: Weight observations by regime probabilities to update $\\mu_i$, $\\sigma_i$, $p_{ij}$\n",
        "\n",
        "This generates a likelihood surface characterized by multiple local optima and regions of high curvature, typical of non-convex optimization problems. Starting points matter enormously (why?). Most practitioners run the algorithm multiple times with different initializations, then pray they've found the global maximum.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **1.3 Financial Applications**\n",
        "\n",
        "It pays to look at where these models shine. Asset allocation becomes regime-aware – you might hold 60% stocks in bull regimes but only 30% when bears prowl. Risk managers use regime probabilities to stress-test portfolios (what if we switch to the bad regime tomorrow?).\n",
        "\n",
        "Option pricing gets particularly interesting. Black-Scholes assumes constant volatility – a heroic simplification. Regime-switching models allow volatility to jump between states, capturing the vol-of-vol that makes long-dated options so treacherous to price.\n",
        "\n",
        "Central banks love these models too. A Taylor rule might work differently in crisis versus normal times. Identifying when the Fed switches from \"patient\" to \"panicked\" can make or break a rates strategy.\n",
        "\n",
        "## **2. Threshold Autoregressive Models**\n",
        "\n",
        "Hidden Markov models keep regimes mysterious. Threshold models take the opposite approach – they link regime changes to observable variables crossing specific levels. It's like saying \"the party's over when unemployment hits 5%\" rather than inferring the party's end from hangovers.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **2.1 Model Specification**\n",
        "\n",
        "The basic two-regime TAR model couldn't be simpler (deceptively so):\n",
        "\n",
        "$$y_t = \\begin{cases}\n",
        "\\phi_{1,0} + \\sum_{i=1}^{p} \\phi_{1,i} y_{t-i} + \\sigma_1 \\epsilon_t & \\text{if } y_{t-d} \\leq \\tau \\\\\n",
        "\\phi_{2,0} + \\sum_{i=1}^{p} \\phi_{2,i} y_{t-i} + \\sigma_2 \\epsilon_t & \\text{if } y_{t-d} > \\tau\n",
        "\\end{cases}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The threshold $\\tau$ acts like a tripwire. Below it, we're in regime 1. Above it, regime 2 kicks in. This piecewise linearity mirrors option payoffs – not coincidentally, since both reflect real-world discontinuities.\n",
        "\n",
        "Note the delay parameter $d$. Markets don't switch regimes based on today's value (that would be simultaneous) but on recent history. Finding the right delay requires careful testing – too short and you model noise, too long and you miss the trigger.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **2.2 Smooth Transition Models**\n",
        "\n",
        "Real regime changes rarely happen instantly. STAR models acknowledge this by introducing smooth transitions:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$y_t = \\phi_1' x_t (1 - G(s_t; \\gamma, c)) + \\phi_2' x_t G(s_t; \\gamma, c) + \\epsilon_t$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The transition function $G(\\cdot)$ acts like a mixing valve. The logistic specification:\n",
        "$$G(s_t; \\gamma, c) = \\frac{1}{1 + \\exp(-\\gamma(s_t - c))}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "creates an S-curve between regimes. Small $\\gamma$ means gradual transitions (markets drift between states). Large $\\gamma$ approximates the sharp TAR switch. As $\\gamma \\to \\infty$, you recover the original threshold model – mathematics confirming that extremes meet.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **2.3 Testing for Non-linearity**\n",
        "\n",
        "Before diving into non-linear modeling, ask yourself: is it even there? Testing for non-linearity when you don't know the form is like looking for your keys in the dark (you know they're somewhere, but where?).\n",
        "\n",
        "The fundamental problem: under linearity, threshold parameters vanish. You can't test the significance of $\\tau$ when it doesn't exist! Davies (1987) called this the \"unidentified nuisance parameter\" problem – academic speak for a statistical nightmare.\n",
        "\n",
        "Solutions exist but require care:\n",
        "- **Lagrange Multiplier tests**: Taylor-expand around linearity\n",
        "- **Neural network tests**: Add hidden units, test their significance\n",
        "- **BDS test**: A general test that detects any departure from IID\n",
        "\n",
        "Each test has power against different alternatives. Running multiple tests isn't cheating – it's prudent."
      ]
