        "In the next example, we will elaborate on the Luukkonen-Saikkonen-Teräsvirta (LST) test.\n",
        "\n",
        "The LST test detects smooth transition autoregressive (STAR) non-linearity by examining whether the autoregressive coefficient changes smoothly with a transition variable. Unlike threshold models that assume abrupt regime switches, STAR models allow gradual transitions between market states - more realistic for financial markets where sentiment shifts progressively rather than instantly. The test works by augmenting a linear AR model with interaction terms between lagged returns and powers of a transition variable (typically the lagged return itself, centered for stability).\n",
        "\n",
        "These interaction terms (xs, xs², x*s³) capture how the persistence of returns might vary with market conditions - for instance, negative returns might decay faster than positive ones, or extreme returns might trigger different dynamics than normal fluctuations. We'll apply this test to S&P 500 daily returns from 2000-2024, comparing results across different market regimes and tracking how evidence of smooth transitions evolves through time. The analysis will reveal that STAR-type non-linearity concentrates during crisis periods, complementing the volatility clustering detected by BDS tests and highlighting the multi-faceted nature of financial non-linearity."
      ]
