        "## **3. Neural Networks for Non-linear Modeling**\n",
        "\n",
        "Neural networks promise to model any non-linearity you can imagine. They deliver on this promise – though sometimes they model non-linearities you didn't imagine and wish they hadn't.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **3.1 Architecture for Financial Time Series**\n",
        "\n",
        "Neural networks function as universal approximators capable of modeling arbitrary non-linear mappings between input and output spaces. The input layer takes your features:\n",
        "$$X_t = [r_{t-1}, r_{t-2}, ..., r_{t-p}, v_{t-1}, ..., z_{t-1}, ...]$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Don't go crazy here. More inputs mean more parameters to estimate from limited data (why is financial data always limited when you need it most?).\n",
        "\n",
        "Hidden layers transform inputs non-linearly:\n",
        "$$h^{(1)} = f(W^{(1)}X_t + b^{(1)})$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The activation function $f$ provides the non-linearity. ReLU ($f(x) = \\max(0, x)$) works well – it's simple, doesn't saturate, and somehow captures market asymmetries. Fancier activations like GELU sometimes help, sometimes hurt. When in doubt, keep it simple.\n",
        "\n",
        "A bit more precisely on depth versus width: deeper networks (more layers) model hierarchical features. Wider networks (more neurons per layer) capture complex interactions within a level. Financial data rarely needs extreme depth – we're not recognizing faces here.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **3.2 Training Challenges in Finance**\n",
        "\n",
        "Financial data hates neural networks. The feeling is mutual.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, non-stationarity ruins everything. A network trained on 2019 data would have been useless in March 2020 (remember when oil went negative?). Rolling windows help but introduce their own issues – how far back is relevant but not ancient history?\n",
        "\n",
        "Second, signal-to-noise ratios make engineers weep. Markets are nearly efficient, leaving crumbs of predictability. Your network will happily memorize noise and call it signal. Dropout regularization randomly kills neurons during training – cruel but effective.\n",
        "\n",
        "Third, fat tails blow up gradient updates. One extreme observation can send your weights careening. Robust loss functions (Huber, quantile) tame the outliers without ignoring them entirely. It pays to look at your gradient norms – if they're exploding, your model is learning the wrong lessons.\n",
        "\n",
        "### **3.3 Interpretability and Risk**\n",
        "\n",
        "The black box problem isn't academic when millions ride on your model. Regulators want explanations. So does your risk committee (why did we lose money yesterday?).\n",
        "\n",
        "Feature importance methods help somewhat. Gradient-based attribution shows which inputs drive predictions. SHAP values decompose individual forecasts. Layer-wise relevance propagation traces decisions through the network. None give the clean interpretation of linear regression – that's the price of flexibility.\n",
